{
  "_comment": "De-brand strangler MOVE-4: home the dep-lint authority's 56 leaf check kernels from the libs/ junk drawer into governance/check/. DESTINATION IS NOT DERIVED FROM FAN-OUT. Measured fan-out puts all 63 of these crates at exactly one external dependent root, but that root is marketplace/facade/dev-cli -- a CLI facade that aggregates 67 check kernels because aggregating is what a facade does. Fan-in from a facade carries no ownership information, so the fan-out-1 rule (one capability owns it -> <capability>/core/) is degenerate here and would have homed 56 governance gates into marketplace/core/. The authoritative answer is already written down twice: specs/capability-registry.json absorbs libs/oya-check-* into meta_dir governance/ (\"The libs/oya-check-* and libs/oya-governance-* crates ARE the dep-lint authority\"), and ADR-0562 section 1 lists \"the dep-lint authority\" among governance/'s residents in both the tree diagram and placement rule 2. The closed registry outranks a fan-out heuristic. ADR-0615 ruling 10 (dev-cli -> ci/, de-CLI'd) is about the CONSUMER and does not relocate the kernels it calls. Face = governance/check/, mirroring the only existing governance/ resident group, governance/corpus/* (ADR-0580); governance/ is a meta root off the runtime ladder, so it takes group/leaf, not the four-layer capability shape. De-brand per ADR-0532/0533: cargo name = group + de-branded leaf (oya-check-cohesion -> check-cohesion), matching corpus-doc-parser; zero collision against the 866-member workspace. 56, not 63 or 72, is a dependency boundary and not a count: 63 of the 72 libs/oya-check-* crates have ZERO in-workspace dependencies, and the batch is exactly the intersection of that leaf set with the fan-out-1 block. The 7 fan-out-1 crates left behind each still depend on a crate that stays in libs/ (oya-shared-backup-kernel, oya-shared-i18n-kernel, oya-shared-compliance-evidence-kernel, oya-shared-realtime-transport-kernel, oya-workspace-members-kernel, oya-governance-adr-shape-kernel, intelligence-catalog-domain), so moving them now would mint a fresh governance/ -> libs/ edge pointing back into the drawer this move exists to drain. The 5 fan-out-2 members (adr-index, license-policy, slo-coverage, brand-residue, cost-budget) and the 4 zero-fan-out members are out of this lane's block. libs/oya-check-* therefore stays in the registry alongside the new governance/check/* glob until the remaining 16 follow, exactly as os/ carried both cloud/cloud-os and os in current_dirs during its transition. WORKSPACE MEMBERSHIP IS NOT AUTOMATIC HERE: the root members array globs the four-layer shape (*/core/*, */ports/*, ...) plus named legacy roots, and governance/ is admitted only by the literal \"governance/corpus/*\" entry -- so this move adds \"governance/check/*\" in the same commit. Without it all 56 crates leave the workspace silently and every member-count gate stays green while reading a smaller universe. Catalog rows re-key with the crates (registry/catalog/oya-check-*.yaml -> check-*.yaml, 56 rows, content-preserving per the ci/ move precedent); governance/ is not one of the fifteen catalog_liveness.workspace_member_globs roots, so no row is REQUIRED at the destination, but leaving the rows behind under their old stems would strand 56 catalog records whose identity is no longer a live crate id. ADR-0562 capability-first placement; ADR-0132 no-grouping (check/ is the single-concern dep-lint authority, not a bundle).",
